WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
Culture may feel exclusively human, but it's been

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.440
observed in all types of animals—

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.820
from chimpanzees to birds.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.560
But what about the humble invertebrate?

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.720
Do fruit flies have culture?

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.960
Researchers tried to find out if fruit flies might have their

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.420
own cultural assets to pass down through the

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:21.920
generations, by looking at mating preferences.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.800
They set up five rules to define whether a behavior was

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.720
learned and cultural.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.840
First, the female fly must learn their mate selection

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.780
preference from watching other female flies choose

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:34.500
a mate.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.960
For example, when an observer female watched a

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.220
second female select a green male to copulate with,

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.340
the observer females overwhelmingly chose a green

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:47.540
male when faced with their own mate selection.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:51.940
Second, the learned behavior must be passed from older

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:53.400
to younger flies.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.460
The observer female selected a green male regardless

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:00.149
of whether they saw copulating females that were older

00:01:00.149 --> 00:01:02.560
or a similar age.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.000
Third, the mating preference must persist.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.740
After showing a female fly a green male copulating

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:12.220
every 15 to 30 minutes several times,

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:16.040
the observer female predominantly chose the green

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:19.260
male—even after 24 hours passed.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.700
Since fruit flies only live for about 40 days,

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:25.800
24 hours is a significant slice of their life.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.800
Fourth, the copying is based on a specific trait.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.620
Even when faced with a green male that had another

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.750
prominent feature, like white eyes or curly wings,

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:42.049
the observer female still usually preferred them over pink males.

00:01:42.049 --> 00:01:45.120
Lastly, the mating preference has to have

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:46.689
a repair mechanism.

00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:50.720
In all the experiments, 80% or less of flies learned a

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.460
mate preference from observation.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:56.360
Without a mechanism to correct for the 20% of flies

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:58.840
that didn't learn, the mate preference

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.899
should theoretically drop down to 51% by the

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.560
third generation.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.900
However, researchers found that learned behaviors

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.600
persisted much longer during the experiment,

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.066
down to the eighth generation.

00:02:12.066 --> 00:02:15.080
Researchers tested to see whether showing several

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:17.650
demonstrations of copulation would lead

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:20.700
to a higher percentage of learned mate preference.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.140
When observer females saw equal proportions of

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:26.799
demonstrator females copulating with green and pink

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.660
males, the observer females had no mate preference.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:34.520
In contrast, if all the demonstrator females were

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:37.810
copulating with green males, a hundred percent of

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:39.900
observer females went with the majority

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:41.740
and chose a green mate.

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:46.020
This exaggerated tendency in the flies to copy the majority

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.420
demonstrates the repair mechanism.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.560
The scientists think their work could shed some light on

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:55.080
how social pressures produce local traditions.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.670
These in turn might drive evolution as they

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:01.260
spread and are passed down in vast nations of humans

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:05.260
or tiny fly colonies in fruit bowls.

